---
import TrustPageLayout from '../layouts/TrustPageLayout.astro';
import { AMAZON_LABEL, MARKET_LABEL, SITE_EMAIL, SITE_NAME } from '../lib/site';
---

<TrustPageLayout
	title="Editorial Policy"
	description={`How ${SITE_NAME} researches pickleball gear, sources product data, and keeps recommendations independent.`}
	canonicalPath="/editorial-policy"
>
	<p>
		This page is the working standard for how we research, write, and update content on {SITE_NAME}. It exists so
		readers know what “recommended” means here — and what it does not.
	</p>

	<h2>What we cover</h2>
	<p>
		We focus on paddles, court shoes, and portable nets that {MARKET_LABEL} players actually search for and can buy
		via {AMAZON_LABEL}, plus supporting categories (balls, bags, apparel, accessories) when they help a complete
		setup. Guides are written for beginners through advanced tournament players, including seniors and players with
		specific fit needs such as wide feet or plantar fasciitis.
	</p>
	<p>
		News posts cover brand launches, sponsorship moves, and tour-adjacent gear context. News is dated and attributed
		(“the brands say,” “industry coverage reported”) and is not a substitute for a full buying guide.
	</p>

	<h2>Research process</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Playing job first:</strong> we start from the problem (control at the kitchen, lateral shoe support,
			a portable net for driveway play) rather than a brand press release.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Public sources:</strong> manufacturer listings, official rules bodies (for regulation topics), and
			reputable industry reporting. We summarize claims; we do not invent quotes, lab results, or review counts.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Catalog integrity:</strong> products referenced in articles must exist in our gear database
			(<code>gear.json</code>) with a stable product ID. Articles pull display data from that catalog — they do
			not duplicate conflicting specs.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Honesty about gaps:</strong> if a weight, material, approval status, or other claim is not confirmed,
			we mark it or leave it out. We do not fabricate “tested” language.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Audience fit:</strong> a “best” pick is always best <em>for someone</em> — we say who, and when a
			cheaper or older model is the smarter buy.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Product IDs and the catalog</h2>
	<p>
		<code>src/data/gear.json</code> is the product source of truth. Components such as product cards and comparison
		tables resolve products by ID. If a product is not in the catalog, we do not invent an affiliate URL or paste
		raw Amazon links into article prose. New products get added to the catalog first, then referenced from guides.
	</p>

	<h2>Prices, ratings, and what we will not claim</h2>
	<p>
		We use approximate catalog price tiers ($, $$, $$$) or clearly labeled “as of / approximate” context when
		useful. We do <strong>not</strong> present those figures as live, authoritative store prices. Amazon and other
		retailers change pricing and availability constantly — always confirm on {AMAZON_LABEL} before buying.
	</p>
	<p>
		We do not invent star ratings, review counts, swing weights, or independent durability scores. Affiliate
		compliance and FTC expectations also mean we avoid “guaranteed lowest price” style language. See the
		<a href="/affiliate-disclosure">affiliate disclosure</a> for commission details.
	</p>

	<h2>Affiliate independence</h2>
	<p>
		Affiliate relationships do not dictate rankings or shortlist membership. We do not accept payment for placements
		in editorial lists. Commission rates are not a ranking input. Secondary retailer links (when configured) are
		optional paths to the same product category — they do not change editorial conclusions.
	</p>

	<h2>Update cadence</h2>
	<p>
		Evergreen guides are reviewed when a material catalog change lands (new flagship models, discontinued SKUs,
		meaningful construction shifts) or when rules/context that a guide depends on changes. We display last-updated
		dates on articles when available. News posts are point-in-time; we may add follow-ups rather than silently
		rewriting history when a story develops.
	</p>

	<h2>Corrections</h2>
	<p>
		If something is wrong — a mismatched product ID, an outdated model generation, a broken claim, or unclear
		affiliate labeling — email
		<a href={`mailto:${SITE_EMAIL}`}>{SITE_EMAIL}</a>
		or use the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. We correct factual errors promptly and note meaningful
		substantive updates in the article's updated date when the change affects buying advice.
	</p>

	<h2>Who is accountable</h2>
	<p>
		Editorial standards are owned by
		<a href="/authors/deco">Deco</a> (Declan Martin), founder and editor. The broader site mission and
		limits of our claims are summarized on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
	</p>
</TrustPageLayout>
